AB  - Mental health issues are a critical concern among college students. Research has consistently demonstrated a significant negative correlation between symptoms such as stress, depression, and anxiety with students' academic achievement, ultimately undermining their educational attainment and long-term well-being. At Institut Teknologi Bandung (ITB), counseling services are available. However, several challenges persist, including limited appointment availability, waiting periods of one to two weeks when slots are fully booked, and the lack of other alternatives in providing quick and easily accessible initial support. This study explore a potential solution through the development and evaluation of a mental health chatbot system that combines Large Language Models (LLMs) with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). This study implemented two architectures: a standard RAG pipeline and an enhanced version using Hypothetical Document Embeddings (HyDE), testing both with closed-source (GPT-4.1-mini) and open-source (LLaMA-3.1-70b) models. The LLM-based evaluation indicates that HyDE generally enhances retrieval performance, although it may decrease when the hypothetical answers are irrelevant. The highest LLM-based evaluation score was achieved by configuration M-02 (gpt-4.1-mini with HyDE applied), with an overall average of 0.9182. The domain expert evaluation confirmed that the chatbot is reasonably capable of producing factual, coherent, comprehensive, and safe responses.
