AB  - This paper presents a retrieval-augmented generation framework that uses model-internal "scope tokens" to decide when to retrieve, which evidence to keep, and how to reconcile multiple sources before answering. Three lightweight controls—retrieve gating, per-document relevance scoring, and cross-document consistency filtering—turn the LLM’s own knowledge boundary into a governance signal for retrieval and context curation. The approach slots into standard multimodal pipelines and reduces wrong answers from irrelevant or contradictory context while preserving helpful evidence for encyclopedic visual and textual queries. Across knowledge-intensive question-answering benchmarks, these controls improve accuracy and robustness over strong multimodal baselines without requiring external annotators or architectural overhauls. By elevating self-knowledge to a first-class signal, the method reframes RAG from passive grounding to active, token-level control of retrieval and fusion.
