AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enhances large language models (LLMs) by grounding generation in external knowledge. However, existing RAG systems typically index documents as independent text chunks and retrieve evidence through semantic similarity ranking. This flat retrieval paradigm overlooks the logical dependencies inherent in service operating procedures, which encode executable workflows governed by step ordering, conditional branching, and causal dependencies, resulting in structurally incoherent evidence and inconsistent multistep reasoning. To address this issue, we propose FlowRAG, a control-flow-guided RAG framework for service operating procedures. FlowRAG represents procedural knowledge as control-flow graphs that capture executable process topology, and organizes supporting evidence around this structure at multiple granularities. In the knowledge construction stage, procedural text is first converted into a grammar-constrained intermediate representation and then parsed into a control-flow graph. In the retrieval stage, FlowRAG performs control-flow-guided path exploration to identify query-relevant step sequences and collect evidence along paths consistent with execution topology. The final answer is generated by conditioning the LLM on the serialized pathstructured evidence under a limited context budget. Experiments on service procedural question-answering datasets demonstrate that FlowRAG outperforms representative baselines in retrieval quality, answer quality, and logical consistency. Compared with existing graph-based RAG methods, FlowRAG delivers stronger overall performance while reducing indexing cost. Our code is available at https://github.com/ColorfulCoco/FlowRAG.
ER  - 

