AB  - To address the challenges of information overload, complex threat assessment, and low human-computer interaction efficiency faced by decision-makers in UAV threat situational awareness, this paper proposes an intelligent question-answering system architecture that integrates deep learning threat assessment models with large language models. The system innovatively combines the quantitative threat assessment capabilities of LSTM-Attention networks with the natural language understanding and reasoning abilities of GPT-series large language models, thereby constructing a four-layer architecture comprising perception, assessment, question-answering, and decision support. By designing a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) mechanism, the system can retrieve threat assessment knowledge bases in real-time and generate professional responses, while employing chain-of-thought prompting techniques to guide the large model in multi-step threat reasoning. A domain-specific instruction dataset covering tactical queries, threat analysis, and response recommendations was established, and the base model was efficiently fine-tuned using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) technology. A test dataset containing 8,000 multi-turn dialogue scenarios was constructed based on the STK simulation platform, with system development and validation completed in MATLAB 2024B and PyTorch environments. Experimental results demonstrate that the system achieves an accuracy of 89.7% in threat question-answering tasks, a consistency of 94.3% with threat assessment results, and an average response time of 1.8 seconds, representing an efficiency improvement of over 80% compared to traditional expert consultation modes, thus providing decision-makers with real-time, accurate, and interpretable threat analysis support.
