AB  - Opioid overdose remains a severe and preventable public health challenge, particularly for individuals with limited access to professional support and local resources. This work presents MOPbot, a grounded, harm-reduction-oriented chatbot designed to provide plain-language overdose education, connect users to local services, and support safer information access in a high-risk public health context. MOPbot combines a large language model with retrieval-augmented generation, planner-based tool routing, and a curated knowledge base containing resources, laws, statistics, and FAQs. The system also incorporates safety-aware design, including emergency detection and refusal of unsafe or illegal requests. A planned three-month pilot study will evaluate knowledge, resource awareness, help-seeking attitudes, usability, and engagement among approximately 50 participants recruited through Memphis nonprofits. These early findings and planned evaluations position MOPbot as a practical framework for community-specific overdose education and resource navigation.
