AB  - Generative AI is rapidly transforming healthcare by enabling smarter tools for clinical decision support, automated documentation, and patient interaction. However, alongside these opportunities, current Large Language Models (LLMs) present significant challenges: they frequently produce hallucinations—information that appears credible but is factually incorrect—and they often depend on cloud-based deployment, raising critical concerns about patient data privacy and compliance with healthcare regulations.As our key contribution, we present RAGClin, a privacy-preserving Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework designed for clinical question-answering. RAGClin combines synthetic medical documents with a local semantic search pipeline to supply verifiable context during inference. Using MiniLM for lightweight dense embeddings and a FAISS vector database for top-k cosine similarity retrieval, relevant context is dynamically fetched and appended to the user query. This combined input is processed by a locally hosted LLaMA 3.1 model via the Ollama runtime, ensuring that sensitive information remains fully local.By grounding generative outputs in domain-specific retrieved knowledge, our contribution mitigates hallucinations, improves factual accuracy, and upholds strict privacy requirements. Preliminary experiments show high semantic precision, reduced hallucination rates, and near real-time performance on both high-performance compute nodes and consumer-grade hardware. These results highlight RAGClin as a practical and secure approach for deploying trustworthy Generative AI in privacy-sensitive healthcare applications.
ER  - 

