AB  - Open Radio Access Networks (O-RAN) require intelligent automation for real-time optimization, yet general-purpose Large Language Models (LLMs) often lack the specialized knowledge needed for telecommunications tasks. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) mitigates this issue by grounding responses in O-RAN specifications and manuals, but existing RAG pipelines typically rely on static retrieval policies that repeatedly query external knowledge regardless of marginal utility. Such indiscriminate retrieval introduces unnecessary latency and compute overhead, conflicting with O-RAN’s strict timing constraints. This paper presents ARGO, an adaptive RAG framework that explicitly optimizes the trade-off between answer utility and inference cost. ARGO models retrieval control as a finite-horizon Markov Decision Process and computes the optimal policy via dynamic programming, yielding a provable two-threshold structure for retrieval, reasoning, and termination. Experiments on O-RAN question answering show that ARGO maintains competitive answer quality while substantially reducing retrieval usage and end-to-end latency. By enabling constant-time, cost-aware decision rules with theoretical optimality guarantees, ARGO provides a deployable foundation for latency-critical O-RAN automation.
ER  - 

