AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) reduces hallucinations in large language models (LLMs) by grounding generation in retrieved evidence, but document chunking, the segmentation step that determines what gets retrieved, is often treated as a peripheral preprocessing decision rather than a core design variable. This systematic literature review focuses exclusively on text chunking strategies in RAG. We searched four major academic databases and screened 358 candidate papers against structured inclusion and exclusion criteria, retaining 17 primary studies published between 2024 and 2025. The reviewed work shows rapid year-over-year growth and is dominated by semantic-based chunking (over half of the studies), followed by rule-based methods, while large-language-model-based and agentic approaches remain underexplored. Evaluation practices are highly fragmented: most studies rely on custom or in-house corpora, public question-answering benchmarks are inconsistently adopted, and reported metrics span classification, ranking, generation, and clustering families with limited overlap across papers, making cross-study comparison difficult. We identify five critical gaps: the absence of standardized benchmarks and unified evaluation protocols, limited exploration of large-language-model-based and agentic chunking, insufficient time-versus-quality trade-off analyses, a lack of methods for structured and multimodal content, and an untested assumption that the corpus is static, since no reviewed study re-segments documents as they change or evaluates chunking on streaming input. These findings position chunking as a first-class design component in RAG systems and outline concrete directions for improving reproducibility, comparability, and real-world applicability.
ER  - 

