AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate extensive potential in text generation, attracting significant user interest and finding wide applications. However, when faced with insufficient knowledge, LLMs may generate information that deviates significantly from factual accuracy, a phenomenon known as the "hallucination" problem. This issue not only diminishes the practical value of LLMs but also presents significant challenges to their further development. Retrieve-Augmented Generation (RAG) addresses this limitation by incorporating external knowledge retrieval to augment the LLM’s knowledge base. However, indiscriminate retrieval can introduce low-quality information, thereby degrading the overall performance of the model. To address these challenges, we propose a novel framework: Heuristic Iterative Multi-source Retrieve-Augmented Generation (HIM-RAG). By leveraging innovative prompt engineering, the framework exploits LLMs’ zero-shot reasoning capabilities and their extensive built-in knowledge bases to produce hypothesized understandings and inferences. HIM-RAG incorporates a heuristic iterative retrieval mechanism, employing an extended hybrid knowledge retrieval toolset—such as knowledge graph reasoning chains and web retrieval technologies—coupled with a Critic Language Model (Critic LM) to filter high-quality information highly relevant to the query. This approach enhances the performance of fact-based complex reasoning tasks. Additionally, a re-ranking module evaluates and prioritizes the generated answers from each iteration, selecting the best-quality response and avoiding overgeneration. This ensures the generated content remains highly relevant to user queries. Experiments on open-domain question answering, reasoning, and fact verification tasks demonstrate that the HIM-RAG framework, applied with two LLM variants, exhibits significant advantages in both accuracy and explainability.
