AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems deployed in medical question-answering applications introduce novel attack surfaces not addressed by existing large language model (LLM) safety mechanisms. This research presents MedRAGShield, a three-tier middleware defense framework designed to detect and neutralise document injection poisoning, hallucination exploitation, prompt injection, and jailbreak attacks against medical RAG pipelines. The proposed system operates across three independently togglable defense modes: a baseline mode with no filtering, a confidence and hallucination gating mode, and a full defense mode augmenting the prior modes with Domain-Aware Sanity Check (DASC) rules that apply medical ontological constraints to generated outputs. This experiment constructs a 40-query adversarial evaluation dataset spanning four attack categories and conducts an empirical evaluation on a clinical pharmacology RAG system built on Llama 3.2:3B and ChromaDB. Results demonstrate that confidence-based defenses alone are insufficient against high-similarity document poisoning attacks, which achieve retrieval confidence exceeding 0.85 while delivering clinically dangerous misinformation, a phenomenon this research terms the high-confidence poisoning paradox. The full MedRAGShield defense reduces Attack Success Rate (ASR) from 13.2% (undefended) to 0.0%, with block rate increasing from 0% to 62.5%, while maintaining sub-second query latency. This work establishes a reproducible evaluation methodology for medical RAG security and identifies domain-specific semantic rule checking as a critical missing layer in current LLM defense architectures. Code, dataset, and defense rules are openly available.
ER  - 

