AB  - While Large Language Models (LLMs) have transformed conversational AI, enterprise settings present significant challenges: cloud-based processing raises data privacy concerns, hallucinations can introduce factually incorrect information, and proprietary enterprise knowledge may go unused. In this paper, we introduce a comprehensive Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system that combines a publicly deployed LLM with semantic document retrieval, enabling organizations to create safe, reliable, and verifiable knowledge assistants. The architecture features intelligent document chunking and generation of semantic embeddings using Sentence Transformers, efficient FAISS/ChromaDB-based similarity search, and locally run LLM inference. Everything from embedding sensitive content to retrieval and generation happens on-premises, ensuring complete data sovereignty and regulatory compliance, while the frontend, deployed on Vercel, offers a user-friendly chat interface with confidence indicators and source citations. We propose a hybrid retrieval fusion mechanism, which is tie-break-aware and calibrated with a confidence score, and report exact quantitative results from a controlled comparison against three baseline systems (vanilla LLM, dense-only RAG, and BM25-only RAG), as well as direct measurement of the reduction in hallucinations on a curated set of 300 queries from an enterprise corpus consisting of 500 documents. The experimental results indicate that the recommended hybrid RAG system reaches an accuracy of 93.5%, reflecting trustworthy and factually accurate replies for business knowledge retrieval. It provides a practical framework for companies aiming to implement reliable, transparent, and privacy-preserving AI systems in regulated sectors for knowledge-intensive tasks.
