AB  - In the current digital world, users rely primarily on digital documents for precise information, but conventional search methods return documents rather than context-aware answers. Despite being able to produce fluent responses, Large Language Models (LLMs) are frequently unable to ground, provide multi-turn consistency, and be reliable in domain-specific tasks. The current RAG pipelines partially solve these problems, experiencing a retrieval discrepancy, a lack of conversational continuity, and little control over factual drift. To overcome these limitations, our model GRAIL-RAG, a document-grounded question-answering framework integrating hybrid retrieval, session-aware multi-turn memory, and multilayer guardrails, is proposed. It uses dense and sparse retrieval with adaptive re-ranking combined with session memory to give out coherent follow-up interactions. Guardrails can assist in providing robustness in retrieval and response quality. The framework is implemented with a modular, LangChainbased orchestration layer that is flexible to combine LLM APIs and retrieval elements. Experimental evaluation across diverse document sets demonstrates improved stability, stronger grounding, and reduced factual drift compared to baseline RAG configurations, making the system suitable for educational, legal, healthcare, and research applications.
