AB  - This study investigates how large language models (LLMs) perform when applied to Islamic question answering. We investigate two models, Mistral-7B and LLaMA-3.2-3B, assessing their ability to answer multiple-choice and short-answer questions derived from a curated dataset of $\mathbf{1 3, 0 0 0}$ Islamic questions. Unlike general-purpose evaluations, our work emphasizes the unique challenges posed by religious content, including the necessity to align with authentic sources and avoid interpretive errors. The study applies prompt engineering, targeted fine-tuning and Retrieval Augmented Generation(RAG) to enhance output quality. Evaluation was conducted using manual and LLM-as-a-judge approach. Results show significant improvements with prompt engineering: LLaMA-3.2-3B improved from 38.78 % to 64.19 %, while Mistral-7B improved from 41.84 % to 70.05 %. Fine-tuning further raised Mistral-7B performance to 68.11 %. Still, the accuracy is not acceptable in such a sensitive field. Hence, we implemented a RAG pipeline to supply grounded context from authentic religious sources. However, retrieval quality, chunking limitations, and embedding mismatch led to frequent irrelevant or missing evidence. These issues also caused mistakes in nuanced religious concepts. As a result, RAG produced only a modest accuracy improvement, remaining far below expectations.
ER  - 

