AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) make mistakes by relying solely on training data. In order to resolve this issue, RAG systems pull in outside information. Standard RAG has flaws such that the system retrieves documents even when retrieval is not needed, and due to this, wrong retrieval introduces new errors that RAG does not fix. In order for the model to check its own output, Self-RAG tried to address this problem with reflection tokens. The approach did help, but one model handling every step still struggles with hard questions. Our approach, Agentic Self-RAG, introduces an orchestration strategy based on rolespecialized agents that are aligned with distinct RAG failure modes. Three agents coordinate through a control flow that is based on varification. The Query Analyzer addresses question ambiguity, the Retrieval Critic addresses evidence incompleteness and contradictions, and the Answer Verifier addresses faithfulness failures. We have implemented explicit verification signals through LangGraph to control iteration instead of relying on learned heuristics. The promary contribution is in this orchestration framework and control strategy, that is built on standard LLM and retrieval components. We tested the approach on the full HotpotQA validation set with 7405 samples. The system reached 54.3 percent exact match compared to 45.2 percent for Vanilla RAG, a 9.1 percentage point improvement. On StrategyQA, we have achieved 68.5 percent accuracy versus 59.2 percent for Vanilla RAG. The system produced 52 percent fewer hallucinations and handled multi-hop questions with improved stability, as reflected in supporting-fact accuracy and evidence recall. Statistical tests were also conducted that show $p$ less than 0.001 with Cohen's $d$ of 0.251 on HotpotQA and Cohen's $d$ of 0.287 on StrategyQA.
ER  - 

