AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities in medical question answering, yet they remain prone to hallucinations—fluent but factually unsupported statements that pose risks in safety-critical healthcare settings. While Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) improves factual grounding, it typically lacks fine-grained verification and targeted correction mechanisms.This paper presents an Explainable Sentence-Level Self-Correcting Framework that explicitly decouples generation from verification and correction. The proposed system first generates responses using a large language model and subsequently retrieves relevant biomedical evidence through dense semantic search. Each generated sentence is independently verified against retrieved evidence using cosine similarity-based semantic grounding. Unsupported sentences are identified as hallucinations and selectively rewritten using an evidence-guided correction model, while supported content is preserved. A bounded re-verification stage ensures consistency without over-correction.Experimental evaluation on curated medical question-answering queries demonstrates a reduction in hallucination rate from 0.41 to 0.09, corresponding to a 71% relative improvement over standalone generation. The framework enhances both factual reliability and interpretability by providing sentence-level transparency and evidence-grounded correction. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of combining post-generation verification with targeted self-correction for improving the trustworthiness of medical LLM systems.
ER  - 

