AB  - India’s legal framework is one of the largest in the world, covering centuries of laws from the Indian Penal Code of 1860 to significant criminal law reforms introduced in 2023. Despite this extensive framework, many citizens, law students, and even practicing lawyers often find it challenging to quickly locate relevant provisions within this vast collection of statutes. Current AI-based legal tools either demand costly cloud API subscriptions or are trained on foreign legal databases that do not apply to Indian laws. We developed the LegalAI Assistant to fill this gap directly. It is a locally-hosted, privacy-focused question-answering system powered by Retrieval-Augmented Generation, which queries an offline collection of 21 major Indian laws and acts. The system merges dense vector retrieval through FAISS with sparse BM25 keyword matching, combining both ranked outputs with Reciprocal Rank Fusion. Before retrieving information, a locally-run large language model enhances the user’s query with legal synonyms to improve retrieval effectiveness. A filename prepending feature addresses a unique Indian issue where identical section numbers across parallel statutes like the IPC and the new BNS confuse language models. All inference is performed offline via Ollama using Llama3 or Mistral, ensuring no data leaves the user’s machine. Testing on 150 manually verified queries yields a Hit Rate of 82.1% and a Mean Reciprocal Rank of 0.71 under top-20 retrieval, along with a 92% reduction in cross-document attribution errors compared to untagged indexing.
