AB  - This study presents a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) chatbot designed for clinical question answering based on the 2024 European Society of Cardiology (ESC) Guidelines for Atrial Fibrillation. Three open-source instruction-tuned language models-LLaMA 3 (8B), Phi-3 Mini (3.8B), and Qwen3 (4B)-were evaluated within a unified retrieval pipeline using MedCPT embeddings, FAISS vector indexing, and a BGE cross-encoder reranker. Performance was assessed across 20 clinical queries using ROUGE-1, BERTScore F1, cosine similarity, and LLM-as-judge metrics (Faithfulness, Answer Relevancy, Context Recall). LLaMA 3 achieved the highest lexical and semantic alignment (ROUGE-1 $=0.4564$; cosine similarity $=0.6647$; BERTScore $\mathrm{F} 1=0.8351$), while Phi3 demonstrated the strongest factual grounding (Faithfulness = 9.75/10). Qwen3 showed competitive semantic performance (BERTScore $\mathrm{F} 1 \approx 0.8187$) and the fastest inference ($\sim 7.9$ tokens/sec) but produced one clinically significant hallucination. All models exhibited reduced performance on figure-based guideline content, highlighting limitations of text-only RAG pipelines. The system was deployed via a Gradio web interface on a Google Colab NVIDIA T4 GPU, demonstrating a practical and accessible approach for guideline-grounded clinical NLP applications.
